    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So if they miss that threshold, it's basically like the signal just doesn't get through efficiently. How much does the total protein quality impact this? Basically, if they're hitting the leucine numbers but the overall amino acid profile is poor, does that interfere with the mTOR signaling or the efficiency of the nitrogen balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The overall amino acid profile is critically important because mTOR requires not just leucine, but a sufficient availability of all essential amino acids (EAAs) to drive complete translation. If key EAAs are limiting, they act as rate-limiters, meaning activation of mTOR might occur, but the machinery cannot build the necessary proteins efficiently. Thus, poor profile quality compromises the efficiency of protein synthesis, even if the leucine threshold is met. A balanced EAA intake ensures all components are available to utilize the mTOR signal for superior nitrogen retention and hypertrophy.",
